# Absolute origin of the deployed site.
# Used for canonical URLs, Open Graph images, robots.txt, sitemap.xml, llms.txt
# and the Organization schema.
#
# Unset, this falls back to the production origin (https://codking.tech), so a
# deploy that forgets it still publishes correct URLs. Local development wants
# the line below; production may set it explicitly or rely on the fallback.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Destinations outside this site (see src/constants/external.ts).
# Install Free is the primary action on every page and points at the Shopify
# App Store listing. Any value left unset renders as a disabled action rather
# than a dead link, so the site stays honest before these are confirmed.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SHOPIFY_APP_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_BOOK_DEMO_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_WHATSAPP_URL=

# The public demo storefront behind "Try Live Demo Store" on the homepage.
#
# A real Shopify store running COD King, so a visitor can take the
# cash-on-delivery checkout themselves. Distinct from NEXT_PUBLIC_BOOK_DEMO_URL
# above, which is the booking action and is reserved for the scheduler — the
# two are never pointed at the same place.
#
# Unset, this falls back to the published store, so the button works out of the
# box; the variable still wins, which is what staging needs.
NEXT_PUBLIC_DEMO_STORE_URL=

# The mailbox behind the contact form (src/app/api/contact/route.ts).
#
# Server-side only — deliberately not NEXT_PUBLIC_*, so Next.js never inlines
# any of it into the browser bundle. These are read in
# src/lib/contact/mailer.ts and nowhere else, and no AWS credential is ever
# among them. Never put a secret in a committed .env file.
#
# Delivery goes through Amazon SES, via @aws-sdk/client-sesv2. With the region
# or the sending identity unset the route answers 503 and the form shows its
# "that did not send" panel pointing at WhatsApp — it never shows a success
# state for a message that was not delivered.

# The SES region. Identities are verified per-region, so this has to match the
# region the CONTACT_EMAIL_FROM identity was verified in — the wrong region
# fails exactly like no region at all. Example: ap-south-1.
#
# Named CODK_AWS_REGION rather than AWS_REGION because AWS_* is a reserved
# prefix on the runtimes this deploys to: Lambda sets AWS_REGION itself, and
# the Amplify console refuses to let you define one. This is the only region
# name the application reads.
CODK_AWS_REGION=

# ── Credentials are not configured here ───────────────────────────────────
# There is deliberately no AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID / AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY /
# AWS_SESSION_TOKEN in this file, and none should be added to it or to the
# Amplify console.
#
# The SendEmail call is signed with whatever the AWS SDK's default credential
# provider chain resolves. In production that is the Amplify SSR compute role,
# whose temporary credentials the chain reads — session token included — and
# refreshes on expiry; that role needs ses:SendEmail on the sending identity
# and nothing more. Locally the same chain reads an AWS profile, an SSO login,
# or plain keys in .env.local if you keep them there.
#
# Passing keys explicitly is what broke production before: role credentials
# arrive as three parts and code that forwards only two of them produces a
# signature SES rejects with UnrecognizedClientException. Leaving the chain
# alone is the fix, so nothing in the application reads a credential variable.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The sending identity. Must be an email address or domain verified in SES in
# the region above, so it has no fallback — a guessed value is a message SES
# rejects. Format: "Display Name <address@domain>" or a bare address.
#
# While the SES account is in the sandbox, CONTACT_EMAIL_TO must also be a
# verified identity or the send fails with MessageRejected.
CONTACT_EMAIL_FROM=

# Where enquiries land. Unset, this falls back to the support address the
# published legal pages already answer on (see src/constants/external.ts), so
# there is one support mailbox in this codebase rather than two.
#
# Set it explicitly on any deployment whose enquiries go somewhere other than
# that address — the fallback is a safety net, not a configuration.
CONTACT_EMAIL_TO=
